TY  - JOUR
TI  - Analysing Structures of Silence in AI-mediated Public Spheres
AU  - Hansen, Ejvind
PY  - 2026
T2  - Philosophy &amp; Technology
AB  - In this paper, I discuss what it will mean for democratic public spheres if more and more content is produced by generative technology based on large language models (LLMs), as represented by OpenAI in the form of Generative Pre-training Transformer (GPT). Although this type of technology can clearly help improve public discourse on a number of parameters, my reading of Deleuze leads me to contend that its heavy reliance on analyses of historical content is a shortcoming. Deleuze has argued that fruitful exchanges require an understanding of how silence helps to constitute what is said. If we cannot work flexibly with silence, what is said loses value. The paper elaborates on this point by discussing how Heidegger’s Fourfold can be read as suggestions for how silence determines what we can say in communicative exchanges. Based on analyses of the technical foundations underpinning AI technology, I argue they tend to reproduce existing structures of silence and that this makes it important to realise that allowing AI to play a greater role in the production of public content still leaves us with the task of challenging existing structures. If we do not face up to that challenge, something will be lost in terms of the ability of the citizen to relate critically and reflexively to the discursive horizons of ongoing public discourse.
