TY  - JOUR
TI  - Practical Mimicry and Modal Integration: Know-How, Large Language Models, and Human–AI Collaboration
AU  - Varghese, Joby
PY  - 2026
T2  - Philosophy &amp; Technology
AB  - Recent advances in large language models have rekindled debate over whether AI can possess know-how. This debate is stalled because intellectualists and anti-intellectualists tacitly assume a monolithic agent model, on which know-how is fixed by a single system’s internal states, independent of architectural organization. Against this assumption, I defend a three-part thesis. First, know-how requires modal integration. A representation of a method must be structured by the causal and normative organization of the task domain to guide success across counterfactual variation. Rival accounts must reintroduce something functionally equivalent. Neither behavioral success, unstructured propositional storage, nor a merely similarity-based modal profile satisfies this condition, though embodied and automatized expertise satisfy it implicitly. Second, because modal integration is architectural rather than substrate-specific, it explains three things. It explains why base-model and immediate-inference generative AI can exhibit practical mimicry rather than know-how where modal integration has not been demonstrated; why intellectualism should be extended into architectural intellectualism without collapsing into a hybrid theory; and why human-AI collaborations under specific coupling conditions generate transactive practical knowledge, a distributed form neither party possesses alone. Third, modal integration is necessary but not obviously sufficient. I flag agential investment as the further condition a complete theory needs and clarify which normative conclusions depend on it. The result is a unified architectural theory of practical knowledge that assesses AI know-how at the appropriate system level, extends intellectualism, and grounds the normative assessment of human-AI collaboration.
DO  - 10.1007/s13347-026-01210-5
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1007/s13347-026-01210-5
